## gcc attribute: format, format_arg
```require
- Compiler: gcc 2.8.0 later
```
### Prior knowledge
Using the GCC compiler option <mark>-Wformat</mark> provides a significant advantage: it allows us to detect potential mistakes, such as argument mismatches, at <mark>compile-time</mark> when calling functions that handle variable arguments like `printf` or `scanf`.  

But what if we could apply the type-checking capabilities of the `-Wformat` option to our own custom functions?  

For example, suppose we wrote the following code:  
```c
{
    "title": "sample source code - format",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>

//[!code highlight]
int report(const char *fmt, ...) {
	va_list arg;

	va_start(arg, fmt);
	vfprintf(stderr, fmt, arg);
	va_end(arg);

	return 0;
}

int main(void) {
	report("%s\n", "report!", "excess");

	return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Compile with -Wformat"
}
---
$ gcc -o format format.c -Wformat
// nothing shows
```
The code above successfully compiles without any warnings, even though the number of format specifiers in the format string passed to the `report` function does not match the number of variable arguments.  

How can we apply the `-Wformat` inspection feature to our custom `report()` function to prevent such mistakes?  
### format attribute
If you look closely at the GCC documentation, you can find an <mark>attribute</mark> called <mark>format</mark>.
```doc
{
    "title": "format (archetype, string-index, first-to-check)",
    "ref": "https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html"
}
---
The format attribute specifies that a function takes <b>printf, scanf, strftime or strfmon</b> style arguments which should be type-checked against a format string.
```
```adcard
```
To summarize briefly, by using this attribute, we can apply <mark>-Wformat</mark> compile-time checks to any custom function we define.
### usage
Now, let's modify the previous example code by adding the <mark>attribute format</mark> so that issues can be detected at compile-time.
```c
{
    "title": "sample source code - format.c",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>

// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((format(printf, 1, 2)))
int report(const char *fmt, ...) {
	va_list arg;

	va_start(arg, fmt);
	vfprintf(stderr, fmt, arg);
	va_end(arg);

	return 0;
}

int main(void) {
	report("%s\n", "report!", "excess");

	return 0;
}
```
If we try compiling again, the following warning will be generated:
```bash
{
    "title": "Compile with -Wformat"
}
---
$ gcc -o format format.c -Wformat

format.c: In function ‘main’:
// [!code warning]
format.c:16:9: warning: too many arguments for format [-Wformat-extra-args]
   16 |  report("%s\n", "report!", "excess");
      |         ^~~~~~
```
The issue in our custom `report` function was successfully detected by the `-Wformat` option.  
```adcard
```
The usage is quite simple.
```c
__attribute__((format(archetype, string-index, first-to-check)))
```
- **archetype**: You can specify functions like `printf`, `scanf`, `strftime`, etc. Depending on the target, you might also use glibc's `gnu_*` or MinGW's `ms_*`.  
- **string-index**: Specifies the **position of the format string parameter** that the compiler uses as a baseline when inspecting the syntax. Note that the index **starts at 1**, not 0.
- **first-to-check**: Specifies the position of the first variable argument to be checked for type and count according to the format string.
### variable argument list
For functions like `vprintf` that take a `va_list` instead of a variable argument list (`...`), the compiler cannot directly check the types or count of the received variable arguments. In such cases, if you set `first-to-check` to **0**, the compiler will handle it appropriately.
```c
{
    "title": "sample source code - format_vprintf.c",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>

// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((format(printf, 2, 0)))
int _va_report(int n, const char *fmt, va_list ap) {
    return vfprintf(stdout, fmt, ap);
}

int report(int n, ...) {
	va_list arg;

	va_start(arg, n);
	_va_report(n, "%s\0\n", arg);
	va_end(arg);

	return 0;
}

int main(void) {
	report(2, "1");
	return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Compile with -Wformat"
}
---
$ gcc -o format_vprintf format_vprintf.c -Wformat

format_vprintf.c: In function ‘report’:
// [!code warning]
format_vprintf.c:13:22: warning: embedded ‘\0’ in format [-Wformat-contains-nul]
   13 |     _va_report(n, "%s\0\n", arg);
      |                      ^~
```
In this case, the compiler only checks for structural errors within the format string itself.  
In other words, since it cannot directly access the count or types of the variable arguments passed, it cannot detect situations where the number of format specifiers and variable arguments mismatch, as shown below:
```c
{
    "title": "-Wformat-extra-args is not detected",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>

//[!code highlight]
__attribute__((format(printf, 2, 0)))
int _va_report(int n, const char *fmt, va_list ap) {
    // can't detect format string error
    return vfprintf(stdout, fmt, ap);
}

int report(int n, ...) {
	va_list arg;

	va_start(arg, n);
	_va_report(n, "%s\n", arg);
	va_end(arg);

	return 0;
}

int main(void) {
	report(3, "1", "2", "3");
	return 0;
}
```
```adcard
```
`strftime` is also used in the same way by setting `first-to-check` to 0.  
```c
{
    "title": "sample source code - strftime.c",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((format(strftime, 1, 0)))
void get_time(const char *fmt, struct tm * tptr) {
    char buf[64];

    strftime(buf, sizeof(buf), fmt, tptr);
    puts(buf);
}

int main(void) {
    time_t tmp;
    struct tm *tptr;

    tmp = time(NULL);
    tptr = localtime(&tmp);

    get_time("%A, %b %d.\nTime: %r..%i?", tptr);

    return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Compile with -Wformat"
}
---
$ gcc -o strftime strftime.c -Wformat

strftime.c: In function ‘main’:
// [!code warning]
strftime.c:19:38: warning: unknown conversion type character ‘i’ in format [-Wformat=]
   19 |     get_time("%A, %b %d.\nTime: %r..%i?", tptr);
      |                                      ^
```
```adcard
```
### function declaration
You can also specify the attribute in function declarations as follows:  
```c
int report(const char *fmt, ...) __attribute__((format(printf, 1, 2)));
```
This attribute is quite old and was already included in the `GCC 2.8.0` release.  
At that time, it only supported two archetypes: `printf` and `scanf`.
```c
{
    "title": "gcc 2.8.0 release - c-common.c"
}
---
static void
init_attributes ()
{
  ...
  add_attribute (A_FORMAT, "format", 3, 3, 1);
  add_attribute (A_FORMAT_ARG, "format_arg", 1, 1, 1);
  ...
}
```
Support for `strftime` was added in `GCC 2.95`.
### format_arg
While examining GCC-related source files or documentation, you might also frequently encounter the <mark>format_arg</mark> attribute.  
This attribute is assigned to functions that process a format string and **return a new format string**.  

For example, it is useful when writing a function that prepends a specific prefix to the original format string or returns a translated format string.
```c
{
    "title": "sample source code - format_arg.c",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>

// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((format_arg(3)))
char *debug_format(char *buf, size_t len, char *fmt) {
    snprintf(buf, len, "[debug] %s", fmt);

    return buf;
}

int main(void) {
    char buf[32];

    printf(debug_format(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s\n"), "arg1", "excess");

    return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Compile with -Wformat"
}
---
$ gcc -o format_arg format_arg.c -Wformat

format_arg.c: In function ‘main’:
// [!code warning]
format_arg.c:13:43: warning: too many arguments for format [-Wformat-extra-args]
   13 |     printf(debug_format(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s\n"), "arg1", "excess");
      |                                           ^~~~~~
```
### conclusion
Personally, I think <mark>-Wformat</mark> is an extremely useful and powerful option because it allows us to catch format string-related bugs—which can lead to critical runtime errors (e.g., memory reference errors, segmentation faults)—early during the compilation stage.  

Therefore, when writing code, it is highly recommended to use <mark>-Werror=format</mark> to escalate format-related warnings into compilation errors whenever possible.  

However, it is easy to forget to assign these attributes when defining custom functions. To prevent this, GCC also provides the <mark>-Wmissing-format-attribute</mark> option.  

In conclusion, when designing a function that takes a format string as an argument, I strongly recommend actively applying the <mark>format attribute</mark> to minimize developer errors and improve software stability.
